Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Furiosa router publishes each usable parallel configuration as its own model
id (`Qwen3-4B-FP8@dp2`), so the picker listed five near-identical rows per model
and gave no sense that they were the same model served differently.

Group them instead: the picker shows one row per configuration as before, but
the focused model now gets a config row — `NPU [dp1·pp1]  dp2·pp1  dp4·pp1` —
and ←/→ moves between them.

Those keys were already wired for effort level and returned early for models
without it, which is every NPU model, so they were dead. Reusing them costs no
new keybinding. cycleNpuVariant returns null for single-config models (all tp32
models, and every non-NPU provider), leaving the keypress a no-op exactly as
before, and the row hides itself in that case.

Only combinations the hardware can serve are ever published — tp is fixed at
artifact build time, pp is unsupported for FXB artifacts, dp x pp is capped by
card count — so everything shown is selectable; nothing needs greying out.

The component is hand-written rather than compiler output; the two touched
memo blocks get their new dependency in freshly allocated slots (_c(84) → 87).

